.TH "KSECRET" 1 "2026-09-29" "toolbelt 0.1.0" "toolbelt manual"
.SH NAME
ksecret \- show a Secret decoded, one key or all
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
ksecret [-n NAMESPACE] [--show-keys] NAME [-- kubectl options]
ksecret [-n NAMESPACE] [-c] NAME KEY [-- kubectl options]
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
A Secret keeps each value in base64, so \fBkubectl get secret \-o yaml\fR shows strings you cannot read. The usual fix is a jsonpath and a pipe to \fBbase64 \-d\fR for every key. \fBksecret\fR decodes every key at once and prints them in aligned columns, with the Secret's namespace, name, type and key count on top.
.PP
Values print as text when they are text. A value over several lines is indented under the first, so a config file stored in a Secret stays readable. An empty value shows \fB(empty)\fR. A binary value, such as a keystore, is not printed. \fBksecret\fR gives its size and the command that saves it to a file.
.PP
TLS Secrets get a summary instead of a PEM block. A certificate shows its subject, the date it expires and the days left, read with \fBopenssl\fR. A private key shows its type and size and stays hidden, since it is the one value you do not want in your scrollback or a screen share. \fB\-\-show\-keys\fR prints it.
.PP
With a KEY, \fBksecret\fR prints that one value and nothing else, byte for byte. That makes it safe in scripts and pipes, such as \fBksecret db\-creds DB_PASSWORD | psql ...\fR. A newline is added only when the output is a terminal. \fB\-c\fR copies the value to the clipboard with \fBclip\fR and prints only how many bytes it copied, so the value never shows on screen.
.PP
\fBksecret\fR only reads. It never changes a Secret.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-namespace NS\fR
The Secret's namespace. The context's own namespace by default.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-copy\fR
Copy the value of KEY to the clipboard and print nothing on stdout. Needs a KEY.
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-keys\fR
Print private keys in full when showing every key.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No header line.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Print each kubectl command before it runs.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show the help.
.SH WHAT EACH VALUE SHOWS
.TP
Text on one line
The text.
.TP
Text over several lines
The lines, indented under the value column.
.TP
Empty
\fB(empty)\fR
.TP
Binary, such as a keystore
\fBbinary, N bytes, save it with: ksecret \-n NS NAME KEY > file\fR
.TP
A PEM certificate
\fBCN=..., expires DATE, N days left\fR, or \fBCN=..., expired DATE, N days ago\fR
.TP
A PEM private key
\fBRSA 2048 private key, hidden, use \-\-show\-keys\fR, and the same for EC and Ed25519 keys
.TP
An encrypted or OpenSSH private key
\fBencrypted private key, hidden, ...\fR or \fBOpenSSH private key, hidden, ...\fR
.PP
Without \fBopenssl\fR, a certificate shows \fBPEM certificate, N bytes\fR, and a private key shows \fBprivate key, hidden, use \-\-show\-keys\fR with no size.
.PP
A single KEY always prints its exact bytes, whatever they are.
.SH SAFETY
.IP \(bu 2
\fBksecret\fR prints secret values. Run it where nobody reads over your shoulder, and not in a recorded terminal.
.IP \(bu 2
Private keys stay hidden unless you pass \fB\-\-show\-keys\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-c\fR keeps the value off the screen. \fBclip\fR holds it in the clipboard until you copy something else.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-v\fR shows the kubectl commands, never the values.
.SH PASS\-THROUGH
.PP
Options after \fB\-\-\fR go to \fBkubectl get secret\fR.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ ksecret db-creds -- --context kind-kind
$ ksecret -n shop shop-tls -- --kubeconfig ~/.kube/lab.yaml
.fi
.RE
.SH NEEDS
.PP
\fBkubectl\fR, \fBjq\fR and \fBbase64\fR, from coreutils. \fBopenssl\fR, optional, for certificate and key details. \fBclip\fR, from this toolbelt, for \fB\-c\fR.
.PP
The account behind your context needs \fBget\fR on secrets in the namespace. Many clusters give that only to admins.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Every key of a Secret
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ ksecret -n shop db-creds
secret shop/db-creds, type Opaque, 6 keys
DB_HOST      postgres.shop.svc.cluster.local
DB_USER      shop
DB_PASSWORD  fake-pass-123
blob         binary, 4 bytes, save it with: ksecret -n shop db-creds blob > file
app.conf     line one
             line two
empty        (empty)
.fi
.RE
.SS A TLS Secret
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ ksecret -n shop shop-tls
secret shop/shop-tls, type kubernetes.io/tls, 2 keys
tls.crt  CN=shop.example.com, expires 2026-12-18, 79 days left
tls.key  RSA 2048 private key, hidden, use --show-keys
.fi
.RE
.PP
With \fB\-\-show\-keys\fR, the key prints as PEM, indented like any value over several lines.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ ksecret -n shop --show-keys shop-tls
secret shop/shop-tls, type kubernetes.io/tls, 2 keys
tls.crt  CN=shop.example.com, expires 2026-12-18, 79 days left
tls.key  -----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----
         MIIEvQIBADANBgkqhkiG9w0BAQEFAASC...
.fi
.RE
.SS One value, for a script
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ ksecret -n shop db-creds DB_USER
shop
$ ksecret -n shop db-creds DB_USER | od -c
0000000   s   h   o   p
0000004
.fi
.RE
.PP
The second command shows that no newline was added when the output went to a pipe.
.SS Copy a password without showing it
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ ksecret -c -n shop db-creds DB_PASSWORD
ksecret: copied DB_PASSWORD (13 bytes) to the clipboard
.fi
.RE
.SS A typo in the name
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ ksecret -n shop db-cred
ksecret: no Secret db-cred in shop. Did you mean db-creds?
$ ksecret -n shop db-creds NOPE
ksecret: no key NOPE in secret shop/db-creds. Keys are DB_HOST, DB_USER, DB_PASSWORD, blob, app.conf and empty
.fi
.RE
.SS What it runs
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ ksecret -v -n shop db-creds DB_HOST
+ kubectl get secret db-creds -n shop -o json
ksecret: secret shop/db-creds has 6 keys
postgres.shop.svc.cluster.local
.fi
.RE
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.PP
\fBksecret: the cluster refused: secrets "db\-creds" is forbidden ...\fR : Your account may not read Secrets in that namespace. That is common and deliberate. Ask for a Role with \fBget\fR on secrets, or use an admin context with \fB\-\- \-\-context NAME\fR.
.PP
\fBksecret: \-c copies one value, name the key\fR : \fB\-c\fR needs a KEY, since copying every key at once would give you one long blob.
.PP
\fBclip could not copy the value\fR : \fBclip\fR found no clipboard. Over ssh or on a server with no desktop, print the value and pipe it where it goes.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
It printed or copied the values.
.TP
1
No such Secret or key, \fBclip\fR failed, or kubectl failed.
.TP
2
Bad usage, such as \fB\-c\fR without a KEY.
.TP
3
kubectl, jq or base64 is missing.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBclip\fR, \fBjwtpeek\fR, \fBcertcheck\fR, \fBkyaml\fR, \fBbase64(1)\fR, \fBopenssl\-x509(1)\fR
